{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And if you say in your heart: How shall we know the word which HaShem has not spoken?</b> You are destined to say this when Hananiah son of Azzur comes and prophesies (Jeremiah 27:16), \"Behold, the vessels of the house of HaShem are being brought back from Babylon,\" while Jeremiah stands and cries out (ibid. verses 19–22) concerning the pillars, the sea, and the rest of the vessels that were not taken into exile with Jeconiah — \"to Babylon they shall be brought with the exile of Zedekiah.\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 18:21) from Sifrei (Sifrei 178). And this derash is because Scripture did not say <b>and if you say in your heart</b>, and it is as I have already mentioned (Ramban on Deuteronomy 4:25), that the Torah indicates through allusions the things that will come to be. But the essential meaning of the verse is to say: how shall we know the word which HaShem has not spoken — so that this prophet be put to death. And He commands that when the thing does not come to pass, then he shall be put to death, for HaShem has not spoken it, and you shall not fear him on account of his wisdom or on account of the sign that he performed, even though the sign and the wonder came to pass.\n\nAnd similarly they should have asked: how shall we know the word that HaShem has spoken, for which we are obligated to heed him in all that he commands? But this is the answer to both questions, for with every prophet we look to the future event that he foretells: if it does not come to pass, we know that he is a false prophet and he shall be put to death; and if it all comes to pass — behold, he is confirmed as a faithful prophet of HaShem, and we are obligated to heed him in all that he commands in the name of HaShem, as it says (Deuteronomy 18:15), <b>unto him you shall hearken</b> — even to transgress words of Torah according to the need of the hour, such as Elijah on Mount Carmel. And this is the meaning of <b>like me</b> <b>like you</b> — that he must first be confirmed as a faithful prophet of HaShem, either through a sign and wonder in the manner stated (Exodus 4:30–31), \"and he performed the signs before the people, and the people believed,\" or by foretelling a future event that comes to pass, for that too is a sign.\n\nAnd thus they said in tractate Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 89a): one who disregards the words of a prophet — his death is at the hand of Heaven, as it is written, \"I will require it of him.\" And they say (ibid.): from where do we know that he is punished? — he was given a sign. But Micah, who gave no sign and was punished — where the prophet is already established, it is different. For if you do not say so, Abraham on Mount Moriah — how did Isaac listen to him? Elijah on Mount Carmel — how do we rely upon him and offer sacrifices outside [the Temple]? Rather, where the prophet is established, it is different.\n\nAnd Rashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 18:22): they were already commanded that if he comes to entice you regarding any of the commandments, you shall not listen to him unless he is established for you as a completely righteous man, such as Elijah on Mount Carmel, for the need of the hour; therefore it says, <b>unto him you shall hearken</b>. And this is not correct in my eyes, for the established status of being a completely righteous man is nothing other than being confirmed as a true prophet for all, through what he previously foretold and which came to pass — and that is his sign, as mentioned in this pericope — or through a wonder that he performed before us, and this is the established presumption of prophets.\n\nOr perhaps Rashi intended by <b>a completely righteous man</b> what the Rav, Rabbi Moshe, wrote in the Laws of the Foundations of the Torah (chapter 7, halakhah 7): when they send the prophet, they give him a sign and wonder so that the people will know that El sent him in truth. And not every person who performs a sign and wonder do we believe to be a prophet; rather, only a person whom we knew from the outset to be worthy of prophecy through his wisdom and deeds, in which he has surpassed all of his generation, and who walked in the ways of prophecy, in its holiness and separateness — and afterward he comes and performs a sign and wonder and says that El sent him — it is a commandment to heed him, as it says, <b>unto him you shall hearken</b>. And it is possible that he performs a sign or wonder while he is not a prophet, and this sign has underlying complexities, yet even so it is a commandment to heed him, since he is a great man and wise and worthy of prophecy, and we maintain his presumption — just as we are commanded to render judgment on the testimony of two valid witnesses, even though it is possible that they testified falsely; since they are valid in our eyes, we maintain their validity.\n\nIn these matters and their like, it is stated (Deuteronomy 29:28): <b>the hidden things belong to HaShem our Elohim</b>, and it is stated (1 Samuel 16:7): <b>man sees with the eyes, but HaShem sees with the heart</b>."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:52:04.733261548Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}